  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-434",
              "name": "Insecure File Upload - Dangerous Extensions Accepted at POST /api/file/upload_stream",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Insecure File Upload - Dangerous Extensions Accepted at POST /api/file/upload_stream"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The file upload endpoint POST /api/file/upload_stream accepts and stores files with dangerous executable extensions including .php, .jsp, .py, .sh, and .htaccess without proper validation, enabling potential remote code execution if uploaded files are web-accessible and executed by the server."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The file upload endpoint POST /api/file/upload_stream accepts and stores files with dangerous executable extensions including .php, .jsp, .py, .sh, and .htaccess without proper validation, enabling potential remote code execution if uploaded files are web-accessible and executed by the server.\n\nAn attacker can upload web shells or executable scripts. If the upload directory is web-accessible and the server executes files based on extension, this leads to complete system compromise (RCE). Even without direct execution, stored malware poses a supply-chain and data-integrity risk.\n\n1. Implement a strict extension whitelist (e.g., only .jpg, .png, .pdf, .txt) and reject all other extensions\n2. Rename uploaded files to a non-executable extension (e.g., .tmp) or random filename without extension\n3. Validate file content using magic bytes/libraries rather than trusting the Content-Type header\n4. Normalize all path separators (both / and \\\\) before basename extraction\n5. Store uploads outside the web root or configure the web server to deny execution in the upload directory\n6. Consider using cloud object storage with presigned URLs instead of local file serving",
                "markdown": "The file upload endpoint POST /api/file/upload_stream accepts and stores files with dangerous executable extensions including .php, .jsp, .py, .sh, and .htaccess without proper validation, enabling potential remote code execution if uploaded files are web-accessible and executed by the server.\n\nAn attacker can upload web shells or executable scripts. If the upload directory is web-accessible and the server executes files based on extension, this leads to complete system compromise (RCE). Even without direct execution, stored malware poses a supply-chain and data-integrity risk.\n\n1. Implement a strict extension whitelist (e.g., only .jpg, .png, .pdf, .txt) and reject all other extensions\n2. Rename uploaded files to a non-executable extension (e.g., .tmp) or random filename without extension\n3. Validate file content using magic bytes/libraries rather than trusting the Content-Type header\n4. Normalize all path separators (both / and \\\\) before basename extraction\n5. Store uploads outside the web root or configure the web server to deny execution in the upload directory\n6. Consider using cloud object storage with presigned URLs instead of local file serving"
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-434",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/434.html"
            },
